WYLSFWT. It doesn't look like a very NetHack-ish acronym, but I'm sure
(given our adoration for such long and unusual ones) that it can easily
be adopted into the lingo.

But why?

Certainly there are no fast-food restaurants hiring apathetic
16-year-olds whose pimples must be itching as much as they are to get
out of the kitchen. It doesn't fit in with the entire Dungeons &
Dragons fantasy setting, does it? But then nor do magic markers or
kitchen sinks, for that matter.

If you define #BURGER on compilation, then you might find one of these
special shops in your wanderings in the dungeon. Why settle with
overpriced delis when you can grab a bite at your local McRodney's or
Ogre King? Even that castle you stumbled upon might in fact serve
small, onion-flavored patties on rolls.

The friendly clerk behind the counter would be glad to service you for
his hourly wage of about 5 zorkmids (soon to be raised to 7.25!). You
can kill him for his meagre paycheck, or just to raid the cash
registers, but otherwise, if you'd rather abide by the law, you can
purchase some rock-bottom priced, artery-clogging junk food, served
warm with a side of fries.

Burgers would give as much nutrition as a food ration, perhaps even
more, but go cold (and go bad) over time, eventually causing you nausea
upon ingestion. On purchase, you can choose between the mundane fruit
juice or several flavours of cola, some of which might have rather
magical (or at least caffinated) effects. Kobold Kola, anyone?

And of course, given the new craze for health foods (or at least those
with an illusion of healthiness), you can opt to purchase a salad or
two. While carnivorous pets go wild for cooked meat, Peanuts doesn't
need to miss out! But if you'd like the full course, you can instead go
for some sugary desserts, straight off of the dollar menu. Sundaes are
always nice, at least until they melt in the heat of Gehennom.
Fortunately, there's always a packed icebox in the back room of these
restaurants, but for employees only!

For the classier adventurers, there's the sushi bar next door, serving
fresh eel wrapped in kelp fronds. Just make sure the unagi is dead
first.



